    "case_id": "632",
    "case_summary": "A 24-year-old woman with long-standing systemic lupus erythematosus and lupus nephritis complicated by end-stage kidney disease (on hemodialysis), and hypertension for 8 years, presented with 2 weeks of malaise and dyspnea. Her medications included mycophenolic acid 360 mg twice per day, prednisone 5 mg daily, and hydroxychloroquine 200 mg daily for 18 months before presentation.\n\nBlood pressure: 134/91 mm HgHeart rate: 128 beats/min (tachycardic)Temperature: AfebrileOxygen saturation: 97% on room airRespiratory rate: 18 breaths/minOther findings:- Jugular venous distension- Mild bilateral lower extremity pitting edema\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Leukocyte: 10.7 x10⁹/L- Hemoglobin: 8.3 g/L- Platelet: 466 x10⁹/L (elevated)- Creatinine: 2.58 mg/dL (elevated)- Blood urea nitrogen: 25 mg/dL (elevated)- Glomerular filtration rate: 23 mL/min/1.73m²- C-reactive protein: 7 mg/L- Complement C3: 77 mg/dL (lowered)- Complement C4: 24 mg/dL (lowered)Imaging Studies:1. Chest Radiograph   - Finding: Enlarged heart2. Electrocardiogram (Figure 1A)   - Finding: Sinus tachycardia and signs of left ventricular hypertrophy3. Transthoracic Echocardiography (Figure 1B)   - Dilated left ventricle with end-diastolic dimension of 6.6 cm   - Left ventricular ejection fraction of 32%   - Global hypokinesis   - Normal wall thickness (interventricular septum measuring 0.9 cm)4. Cardiac Catheterization   - Finding: Normal epicardial coronary arteries5. Endomyocardial Biopsy (Figure 1C and 1D)   - Largely unremarkable on hematoxylin-eosin-stained sections   - Toluidine blue-stained sections: scattered vacuolated fibers   - Ultrastructural examination showed increased mitochondria, membranous profiles, lysosomes, and lipid vacuoles",
    "frontier": [
      "B1",
      "B2",
      "B3"
    ],
